35dd378-61e0-57d2-bf30-b547652ac1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086971} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2ef318-0c0f-5ce1-9057-c9cd4b80dfee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086972} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7b4677-b5c3-51e5-95e5-657eb0a28e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086973} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec2efe8-b979-54cc-a27f-528cf8dbba1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086974}] :answer-field/id #uuid "94a7e764-e67d-5112-9873-85a6e14dc106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086969}] :question/id #uuid "38d33573-721b-5c42-99c6-39f5804f5848" :question/math-academy-id "q-80690" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = (x)/(x(x - 2))$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d24de3f8-0012-5833-9d24-f3e6cc246135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d24de3f8-0012-5833-9d24-f3e6cc246135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232487} :answer-field/id #uuid "847bb467-9bef-57c4-b13d-4741ccbfc6e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232486}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d09a218-37ee-56a7-9539-64f2a5b50504" :question/math-academy-id "q-278536" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707649a0-0233-500b-aace-11a761c5191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707649a0-0233-500b-aace-11a761c5191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232489} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea98157-b5fc-5648-86c8-678280c80d9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e1e56fe-2c76-5e2f-82a8-c8d18f84edaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-278543" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234020 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9da44686-a271-5d67-9dd0-7fb4a1f7283c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39802" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{{x}^{2}(x+7)-(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{({x}^{2}-1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+1)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-1)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+7)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf4edc2-58db-5d46-80e0-a7c8624e48ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf4edc2-58db-5d46-80e0-a7c8624e48ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d228aeb4-71ac-53dc-81f5-63488c7179c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e4ca48a-2999-5392-a60c-be71cb24f7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-269990" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+3}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea1d276b-e77e-5cde-9521-ff30bc6339be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea1d276b-e77e-5cde-9521-ff30bc6339be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293845} :answer-field/id #uuid "399a722a-55f8-57cc-8ae2-0074bc503803" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aff4a32a-0112-57be-8fab-aa8ac02ff1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-278535" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+2)(\\cancel{x-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc5bed91-b8c8-5871-b6be-0c61c8ab9057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc5bed91-b8c8-5871-b6be-0c61c8ab9057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293919} :answer-field/id #uuid "368f35c1-f002-5520-85cd-8a682f5c9611" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd432d19-3c99-5837-b724-f7d07317cf6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd432d19-3c99-5837-b724-f7d07317cf6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293671} :answer-field/id #uuid "440128d5-7483-59ac-865c-25e6bd35bb30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93ca9dc7-95c6-5564-8115-405f90e6185c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278545" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-1)-4(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}-4)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{(x-2)(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c5905a-5f25-523a-ac65-98f8c0df5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c5905a-5f25-523a-ac65-98f8c0df5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8ff405-12ed-5fc1-921e-7aa13337b0fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294759}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61a7812d-e909-52ca-b959-ce3ac589b203" :question/math-academy-id "q-278556" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{(x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cecb66-e549-5c1c-ad09-78a83dd33eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324042} {:answer/id #uuid "55df2ba0-f2c6-5604-9a35-63997cf52830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324686} {:answer/id #uuid "cb249387-39bb-5bca-aeb9-bb3d6e5d3f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325733} {:answer/id #uuid "e80c846d-e256-503b-bd6e-c02635e34775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0231bbc-1648-5f25-943c-15ee288b5418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07cecb66-e549-5c1c-ad09-78a83dd33eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324042} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e143c6-8b95-56da-82eb-2acad7352c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd01e245-cc8a-5763-b4a7-8579762d92bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-39804" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{3}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-1{)}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x+5{)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-5.$ Therefore, $x=-5$ is the only vertical asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2964b3dc-2d94-539a-946c-a30b2755e2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324304} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6cc3a3-58f1-5d26-8673-5d478b6c9cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324724} {:answer/id #uuid "73ff3097-7a21-5a24-ac11-6c28413a6ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324935} {:answer/id #uuid "932a43ae-c4e3-506f-b284-5316e420c58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325221} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e97693-64f6-596d-9b4a-111e079d80af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "932a43ae-c4e3-506f-b284-5316e420c58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325221} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f771b5-672e-5778-ace6-2b8c22fa7988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "720a20e0-eaeb-515c-b041-a17373ebe5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39931" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{{x}^{2}\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e616195-c20c-5ce6-a774-a9d27760ce9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345468} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbadb36-36cf-57bb-b77c-60cf2f937db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346153} {:answer/id #uuid "91cba78e-fa38-55f5-8f5a-e2ea6d4d0101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346626} {:answer/id #uuid "a473e00b-5a25-5f34-8225-eb11ceb36777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346779} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e0eef-d46c-5409-9e99-5a2584e7abcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cba78e-fa38-55f5-8f5a-e2ea6d4d0101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346626} :answer-field/id #uuid "2918e306-8491-5fc4-93b6-c33073f50077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dad69b3-29eb-58b2-9a73-eefa5740f30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80691" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{x\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087544 :question/id #uuid "f4879578-ac07-54f8-b00f-5ab651ae240a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5301" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049153 :topic/math-academy-id 441 :topic/title "Factoring Sums and Differences of Cubes"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334 :topic/math-academy-id 425 :topic/title "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050827 :topic/math-academy-id 2337 :topic/title "Further Factoring of Polynomials Using GCFs"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24b3ce4f-f02a-5973-8510-abddd72abd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324272} {:answer/id #uuid "27e02c0c-7e36-5287-8af8-5b938f095220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324290} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c52696-ba99-57ea-8f2e-c13d827516e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325525} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ff13b6-5212-585e-8824-169927916126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325815} {:answer/id #uuid "fa94debb-5a3f-5611-9395-ed887d33efbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e02c0c-7e36-5287-8af8-5b938f095220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324290} :answer-field/id #uuid "40259e5d-4cd1-55c7-8aff-27f6b5a4499e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326324}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bac7c54b-040c-5d96-8fcc-40e2d221cff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39795" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $2x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3a4c00-8634-58fc-90d6-3294ac3c3371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327471} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c362b-0329-5e15-9f0d-4bb67c042f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328331} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b8294a-f4a8-5a82-a733-0419b620a731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,x=0,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328625} {:answer/id #uuid "c933dda2-ec84-5062-ac4e-5f530086befd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8cee54-9108-570e-b47f-cbdac909560a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683930f5-7231-57ef-90a9-236b0f94178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4694" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe654d7-6432-5cc0-b1f6-df2c2b01d27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345899} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb89589-5f3e-556f-8392-084981699059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346024} {:answer/id #uuid "778231e9-1503-5fe8-97dc-d51787729671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346401} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec274e4-80d6-5f09-bde5-9e5dd10197d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346465} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bf79fa-b327-5731-b2d4-26beca27d1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb89589-5f3e-556f-8392-084981699059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346024} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ede0ea-63a7-5cb0-bb81-4c437869eaec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348010}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90dc5317-bc84-5136-bb1e-99a7e0621f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80679" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.\n$$\n\nNote that the above equation has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =16-20 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233171 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6959c63-587b-5482-a15e-ffcddfea0d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-113523" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x-1)+3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd9b367-054b-5790-ace7-f4b19449eb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffd9b367-054b-5790-ace7-f4b19449eb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232491} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec6d923-f04a-5e6b-8731-9e140c7c04b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232490}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28775c75-5409-5ea8-bed7-941c85b2b6e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-278551" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{2}(x-1)+(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+1=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234067 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c29f5a25-a27e-5aa7-83e3-1b45bff33614" :question/math-academy-id "q-4698" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(8{x}^{3}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x[(2x)^{3}+{1}^{3}]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the sum of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- Solving $2x+1=0$ gives the solution $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-2x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =4-16 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234422 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93c6c7f3-46fa-5107-aed4-196cf2a60bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80680" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}+3x+4=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =9-16 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d506aa-841e-5c5b-a1bf-5ac09f60017e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244603} {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940} {:answer/id #uuid "65c64b8c-1872-5671-805d-e70b0c6511d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245211} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1ce093-9dd6-5b3f-aef8-928fca896ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245542} {:answer/id #uuid "ede102f7-ea3c-59b6-a3e1-e4bb8edb3ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83b8a24-f8de-5be9-ab19-5920066dd724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "163eeae3-71ad-5b66-a0aa-4b05d346c12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113525" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01eca7f-16b5-518e-b8bd-7e734e5b4985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01eca7f-16b5-518e-b8bd-7e734e5b4985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a220a84-d2be-57bd-97d7-693834b3b0b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6d17235-f50b-5069-894a-be17656793ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-270003" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5x-20}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5x-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-4)+5(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}+5)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+5)(x-4)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-4=0$ gives the solution $x=4.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+5=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd09b302-f2ca-548f-87d3-3d4055d98ae4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292519}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b59dc04-b4de-517d-b908-41d7f801e2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-270004" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2({x}^{3}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{2({x}^{3}-{1}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-1)=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102836a1-9ad7-5910-882e-215f9e14dbcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333137} {:answer/id #uuid "31b9e16b-6bcb-5bbb-9138-174047d6690f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333445} {:answer/id #uuid "a33ef2aa-239f-5c75-8c30-e1d0699850fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334423} {:answer/id #uuid "e3664c3d-5247-52ed-824d-0d47b62cf94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334969} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ae6dd4-cd83-5007-ae93-ff5a0f083e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8ae6dd4-cd83-5007-ae93-ff5a0f083e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335168} :answer-field/id #uuid "8004c65d-a62f-59aa-85b4-bdb65ced464c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335484}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3613299d-7197-59d6-84af-a80c57279c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-6912" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{3{x}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31037d7d-615f-586a-8606-01f1b4558592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345784} {:answer/id #uuid "4aeb91f0-cd53-5d4d-886c-fb0fd62c216e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345993} {:answer/id #uuid "72812abe-a3ca-5de6-b805-36c4e38ad777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346366} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe36be7-496a-56d8-8d43-bee44a476d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346750} {:answer/id #uuid "e47b15d6-db10-5d9f-9cc1-04b2bdf4db96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31037d7d-615f-586a-8606-01f1b4558592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9c99d4-e1f0-5d10-83b9-846a389a4e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e336039-4f92-550b-b76c-a2a62662e884" :question/math-academy-id "q-80676" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+4(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+4=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a12e93a-5479-5bc8-a172-5f001b81680d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399797} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd56062-f5eb-5d51-97c9-2401cc2382c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399798} {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} {:answer/id #uuid "060d6765-1a4c-5cef-9036-baa44df95b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399800} {:answer/id #uuid "0532888d-2587-5d82-aa30-da64f92b3ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de2cff7-92c8-5c70-9102-18ed3f31a5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "046b8096-a4b3-5639-81fd-05cfaf3fcc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-113527" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+6(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+6=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087546 :question/id #uuid "51ff19a3-5c58-5c26-9c29-14ffc7d284db" :question/math-academy-id "e-5304" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0{)}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048272 :topic/math-academy-id 428 :topic/title "Factoring Cubic Expressions by Grouping"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0b663c6-7296-5416-9279-89c8726a7169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085657} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9114cb-d5f9-5e25-a50c-4703cac865b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085658} {:answer/id #uuid "f04f942f-4bb2-54f4-abe5-95b857ace3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085659} {:answer/id #uuid "0bbb273c-9bc2-55f5-8dc3-b1f51f218cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085660} {:answer/id #uuid "0cecd8b6-06dd-545d-b54e-9bbe30569873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 0, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085661}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a594d63e-4a4f-5e8e-b954-85fb8c9bd564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085656}] :question/id #uuid "658facf5-be39-5c7a-8984-576062c5d409" :question/math-academy-id "q-39927" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{x}{x^{3} + x^{2} + x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ee625a-e88b-5839-8eb5-cf2ea241dae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085777} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc595a-a08c-502c-9e7f-3d082b50a538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085778} {:answer/id #uuid "04625b17-aa0d-540f-a752-c4761d00a508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085779} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0fc964-47c0-5489-bcac-9754e4e48f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085780} {:answer/id #uuid "71472aac-8ea6-50d8-b51b-f50282c3497c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085781}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7a417943-12c6-5645-8b1a-de1e21902255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085776}] :question/id #uuid "9804f06a-3a1e-50fe-a8f6-ab3f28486a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-4695" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{3} - x^{2} + x - 1}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233172 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ce523e6-de7a-5c10-9dbd-54e117565b86" :question/math-academy-id "q-113547" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x+1)+(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+1}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233173 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e3a4d1b-ccf5-5eb8-a09b-9826eb08d075" :question/math-academy-id "q-113550" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{(x-3)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b06798-2160-5123-adeb-8fac4e25409f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244493} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4901-52db-5ea7-b778-613db328f9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244797} {:answer/id #uuid "95e4ede5-330a-5d5d-b519-47ca590e8dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245627} {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246321} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4b0856-0eaf-556c-a63b-bf6bfda2eaae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246321} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5440fc-dc76-5e79-91b4-ff690a389de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daec1f65-9c08-5df4-939b-bebb2395a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-113545" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341cefcf-6aa7-5dd0-8741-16a1e49c1126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324389} {:answer/id #uuid "b527320d-de25-5e52-98bb-3d68c7423f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325527} {:answer/id #uuid "d38f3c2a-a49e-5897-801c-424300e7c8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325808} {:answer/id #uuid "d670e8d0-18bf-5af2-8bdf-5ce021e9152d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325837} {:answer/id #uuid "f34e9c7e-81fd-5622-b802-8d9eedc6bd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "341cefcf-6aa7-5dd0-8741-16a1e49c1126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324389} :answer-field/id #uuid "08795b3d-c8a6-5731-9194-35bf6c829513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f92ee8b-33fa-5343-9c16-73df2f465b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-39934" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{x({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+2x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+8=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(8) \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d801c1c-70ef-575e-a8ce-0cf042036ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345870} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b5cb8-178a-5fd6-856c-24bea81fccc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346879} {:answer/id #uuid "c858a68d-354f-5009-8590-cab42e410104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347095} {:answer/id #uuid "d8905902-e64e-5add-b785-c52f97aa4e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347258} {:answer/id #uuid "d89d09f7-625f-5ed9-981b-246d07b3adc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d801c1c-70ef-575e-a8ce-0cf042036ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345870} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f66b65f-a57a-5bd8-9c0c-795c2010f044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347636}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fbfa1c-696c-569c-8a32-b9f903615eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80696" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a272db-6815-53ec-87da-5a281c0b5932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396109} {:answer/id #uuid "81bc4acc-cf96-53dd-9604-97c51e416cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396110} {:answer/id #uuid "d3972294-8bb0-593a-9b73-d4e09973c691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396111} {:answer/id #uuid "4649603f-3d59-5567-ab7b-c75a268bb6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3a157a-5fe4-53f2-9241-98666b5d0e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b3a157a-5fe4-53f2-9241-98666b5d0e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396113} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc4a2be-1828-5c44-9645-c520108c6c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396108}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9bdca2-12f3-5135-add9-f6cee60b6017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399804} {:answer/id #uuid "ea637b56-a1b6-530a-b8ad-0581d7725118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{3},x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399805} {:answer/id #uuid "6caf38b1-8d42-5322-9446-2276f113b03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399806} {:answer/id #uuid "88004c88-6f69-578a-840b-c594bbb79e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399807} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9f7db0-7e00-5308-89b8-2a0dcddb1466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a602f803-9ee4-55ae-ba4f-d88ab6ec6a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402697} {:answer/id #uuid "d1acf715-1373-5a06-b01b-ab3b303c535d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbc63c9-13bb-57db-8074-636542eaac49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402699} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7f76d-26a7-52c7-993f-99c8712a83c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402700} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e77536-5db4-551c-9973-483a9864ffa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402696}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab721a7c-ebb0-5452-b247-5d3816198bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\sin x)y = 1 + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084927} {:answer/id #uuid "b319152d-c291-5494-a461-4791daa6c062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\cos x)y = 1 + 3\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084928} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084929} {:answer/id #uuid "2a507547-4026-5e0f-ae6c-972cd79efc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2} = (2\\sin x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084930} {:answer/id #uuid "606289fa-36e0-57ab-b7c2-57b9dbbb4540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084931}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e534d32-a3b5-5b67-a30a-d8fb0702e472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82a4b2-58af-556c-a366-f92624768704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401435} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a26a57-987c-5668-b531-e2f3db2b75bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401436} {:answer/id #uuid "b6972414-a235-5987-855e-567ba571b605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401437} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cc03c-cea4-583d-b68b-e5c8622f76e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}=(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef647b19-79e2-5b54-b8df-1bf5fe9db9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d56ade3-8c2b-558b-8dee-d6c16408ae98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348562} {:answer/id #uuid "19d75fc4-d565-5247-a3d2-0dd1d71481b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348663} {:answer/id #uuid "33f582cb-d125-5a7e-bc13-6fd636ea22e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348913} {:answer/id #uuid "a15aa42d-fc0c-537c-988d-61613c47dc74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349879} {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ee4a69-5c37-5b5b-9d83-fd6eb1f2f193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16600393-5943-5c7c-83fa-73f280d9f496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-2x}y-{e}^{-x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235954} {:answer/id #uuid "28fd1a90-bb3f-5f34-a198-d64604e7ece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236074} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5f3f74-0a23-5401-aa89-9d12635660cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y-{e}^{-2x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236394} {:answer/id #uuid "71948203-de54-56d1-9f56-93d9391d2c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236538} {:answer/id #uuid "fca9fcb4-f28c-5421-8fe4-2f2bc1857dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fd1a90-bb3f-5f34-a198-d64604e7ece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236074} :answer-field/id #uuid "701c817f-730c-54ca-8fa6-f662db8b263d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237586}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee14253e-e208-56bc-9e49-57c918963c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-103349" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y & =x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2{e}^{-x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{e}^{-2x}x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2{e}^{-x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{-2x}x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8ddc79-cd90-5a3a-92b9-66917add9af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236301} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa8218-25c6-5f9d-90b8-aac5aa59b9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236644} {:answer/id #uuid "928963ed-bc3c-5514-8870-62a00c67947b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236747} {:answer/id #uuid "b883f316-044b-5d72-a310-71831ab4f8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236997} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027} :answer-field/id #uuid "250ff475-83b4-5e4f-9a44-f3c56416dc2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237486}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b89344e-126d-544e-88e0-0303a3eae594" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bcc262-1642-5072-847b-f965c83d8d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282135} {:answer/id #uuid "6dee7921-ed08-59bd-8f27-4deeb4c2708b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282631} {:answer/id #uuid "721c80a1-3d3f-59bd-b10a-ec28f6a3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} {:answer/id #uuid "78540369-9d2a-5cf2-9a31-e8d914b35aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282721} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa81d24-8383-5421-81be-7c245419023a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "721c80a1-3d3f-59bd-b10a-ec28f6a3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} :answer-field/id #uuid "71914272-d79b-5413-b8f9-d3326e0d438c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "157e16d4-eb84-5272-a4e6-39b4e7ee7766" :question/math-academy-id "q-16396" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={y}^{2}x\\,,\\,y\\ne 0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb601d3-81c5-5a96-8fc8-ebd341ceb45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} {:answer/id #uuid "1b28838c-5554-561f-8d1f-b421c2cfbbcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281936} {:answer/id #uuid "c21f19ed-5569-516f-809d-8429394e2585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283377} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ad13ec-6b04-54a8-8096-a847fbcb5705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283535} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b0a236-a0ee-50c6-beda-f5362d816f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb601d3-81c5-5a96-8fc8-ebd341ceb45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bbd239e-b8c4-5069-b4e1-e9313244312e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ce4583e-c99f-532c-8f96-a07269c60d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16401" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,y & ={{1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646fdce-fd3c-56a7-8137-632d215be116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345404} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9afd84-fe8c-56a0-89e1-00f6ac764479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346123} {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} {:answer/id #uuid "d204e6a5-02d7-5404-bddf-a96974b0383d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347190} {:answer/id #uuid "d895b1af-8150-5cb7-9f48-017f2ef90f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ddf3aa-733e-5972-8973-46d76ff540e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347812}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f284d9d4-47c8-54c3-aa2b-11e7ba22d40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79697" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{x+y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345388} {:answer/id #uuid "139be62e-1605-5f69-b8d1-6b7a2554416d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e94f02d-431a-5153-8655-00b4a87c74f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345883} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef9fe74-cdac-5d6d-8da5-effef6765981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346026} {:answer/id #uuid "50906fb5-5aea-54da-a635-e0e0c3771435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5xy=-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345388} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ad19c6-417f-54a2-8eac-d9c3b03caa0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347862}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a5eea4d-3d1c-5658-8171-517491bf7c77" :question/math-academy-id "q-79699" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-5{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23ea584-4a5e-5d3f-b114-caef1a56d6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404261} {:answer/id #uuid "178d8403-eb54-593d-abc3-5b52a35b7885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404262} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26e4a3-b2b4-56a4-a778-0012ee7e2935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404263} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4fc3c-40eb-5ddc-a361-e415766dbedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404264} {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d75aab-799f-5138-9f8f-42b050f51ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404260}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}]}
